[c0012] **c0012 is in `review`.** Committed as `97591f7` (four files; the unrelated working-tree changes on `bevy019` are untouched). `cargo build` clean, 218 tests pass.
[c0012] What landed:
[c0012] - **`level::campaign`** — `save_level` / `save_campaign` via `std::fs`, plus `LevelSaveError` and a `CAMPAIGN_HEADER` constant so appending to `campaign.ron` doesn't eat its comments (pinned by a byte-for-byte round-trip test).
[c0012] - **`editor::save`** (new) — the write, the three validation rules, and a third panel under the history bar: `Save` / `Campaign` buttons, `Ctrl+S`, and a report of what the last save did with a line per complaint.
[c0012] - **The reload guard** — `LastSave` holds what was written; `editor_watch_the_file` now drops the undo history only when the file comes back saying something *else*. Content comparison rather than a flag, because the watcher fires a debounce later, after the author may have painted more cells.
[c0012] Two judgement calls I made rather than blocking on, both flagged on the card:
[c0012] 1. **Enrolling in the campaign is a separate press from saving**, and a new level gets an auto-picked `levelN.ron` (there's no text entry in the game). The alternative — save auto-appends to the campaign — is a two-line change if you'd rather have it, but it would mean the editor can no longer produce a scratch level.
[c0012] 2. **Saving a level file loses its comments.** RON drops them on parse, and a save is a rewrite. `level0.ron`, `level5.ron`, `level6.ron`, `conveyor.ron`, `simple1.ron` and the two demos all carry hand-written notes that will go the first time the editor writes them. `campaign.ron` is protected because the editor touches it routinely; the level files aren't. Worth knowing before you open a shipped level in the editor and hit `Ctrl+S`.
[c0012] I couldn't eyeball the panel in the running game — reaching the editor needs keystrokes this session can't send. The layout is asserted against the same rectangle functions the settings panel and history bar use, including a test that every row sits inside the panel, but the text-fitting is estimated rather than measured: report lines get two rows of height to wrap into, and the button captions were shortened to fit the column. Worth one look when you next run it.
